Background

Security cameras often have an Ambient Light Sensor (ALS), which measures the
amount of light around the camera and triggers a "night mode" when the ambient
light is too low.

You've been given an ALS that reports sensor readings using the following struct:

    struct SensorReading {
        int      status;     // ERROR / NO_CHANGE / VALID
        float    lux;
        uint64_t timestamp;  // time of reading, microseconds (gettimeofday)
    };

To get a sensor reading, call:

    struct SensorReading read_next_sample();

This function will do one of two things:

  - The amount of light does not change within one second: the sensor returns
    NO_CHANGE in the status field. A total of one second elapses before the
    function unblocks.

  - The amount of light does change within one second: the sensor returns
    VALID in the status field and populates lux and timestamp. A variable
    amount of time elapses before the function unblocks, between 0 and 1 s.

  Note: the first call to read_next_sample() always returns a valid value
  without blocking.

  (Seen in the harness, not said out loud: status can also be ERROR, with
  probability 1/10000 per call, returned immediately with lux/timestamp
  left uninitialized.)

Working with blocking functions is hard and not thread-friendly, so we want to
wrap the ALS function in a non-blocking, thread-safe way.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.
  Design and implement an API that allows users to read the most recent valid
  lux value. The API should be non-blocking and thread-safe.

      void  init_recent_lux(void);        // (the pad's stub returned void)
      float get_most_recent_lux(void);

Part 2.
  Design and implement an API that allows users to read lux values from any
  time within the last 10 minutes. Your API should be non-blocking and
  thread-safe.

      float get_lux_at(uint64_t timestamp);   // timestamp in the same clock
                                              // as get_timestamp()

  The value "at time t" is the lux of the most recent VALID reading whose
  timestamp <= t (the light did not change after it, or we would have been
  told).

  Interviewer's hint during Part 2 (what they said, roughly):
    "Usually people talk about a CIRCULAR QUEUE first. Then, thinking about
     it more, a LINKED LIST. And the most optimized one is a RED-BLACK TREE."

    (1) circular queue  fixed memory, O(1) append/evict
                        - must guess capacity (sensor has no rate limit)
                        - linear scan O(n)  (binary search -> O(log n) if sorted)
    (2) linked list     no capacity guess, grows with the data
                        - O(n) lookup, malloc per sample, cache misses
    (3) red-black tree  keyed by timestamp: dynamic size AND O(log n)
                        insert / floor(t) lookup / delete-min eviction,
                        out-of-order inserts are fine. Linux kernel rbtree:
                        hrtimers, CFS, epoll.
    They want the progression and the reason for each step, not one answer.

